  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe latest radar and satellite imagery reveal a surface cold \nfront that has exited south of the area this afternoon, with \nresidual cloud cover lingering across the region. Cloud cover is\nexpected to quickly erode by this evening as an area of high \npressure briefly builds south into the Great Lakes. \n\nAnother cold front will move east through the area on Sunday, though \nprecipitation chances should be limited or negligible with a large \narea of mid-level dry air in place. Behind this front, a stronger \narea of Canadian high pressure will build south into the region, \nushering in cooler and drier air. The highest confidence for the \nmost widespread frost threat appears to be Monday night into Tuesday \nmorning when the high is directly overhead, resulting in efficient \nradiative cooling. The frost potential will continue Tuesday night \ninto Wednesday morning, though the area may become more limited and \nconfined to sheltered areas/inland Northwest Pennsylvania as warm \nair advection gradually increases from the west. \n\nAnother weak cold front may cross the area by the end of the week, \nthough the latest guidance suggests this may be another mainly dry \nfrontal passage with ample mid-level dry air in place.",
